EXPERIMENT 42 – REGRESSION TESTING FOR CONTENT MANAGEMENT SYSTEM
AIM
To implement regression testing for a content management system after updates. Regression testing confirms that new features, fixes or patches have not damaged previously working functions.
ALGORITHM
1. Start and identify the changes made in the CMS update.
2. Select existing test cases covering the changed modules and dependent modules using impact analysis.
3. Prioritize test cases as High, Medium or Low.
4. Execute selected test cases on the updated system and compare results with the earlier version.
5. Identify dependent modules and rate their regression risks.
6. Execute high-priority test cases first and report differences as regression defects.
7. Report failed test cases, retest after fixes and stop.
INPUT
Impact Analysis of the Update
Changed Component	Impacted Modules	Risk
New editor	Pages, media library, publishing	High
Login security patch	Login, user roles, password reset	High
Theme update	Front-end pages, menus	Medium
SEO plug-in update	Page meta tags, sitemap	Medium
Unchanged modules	Search, comments	Low

Regression Test Suite
TC ID	Module	Test Description	Priority	Status
RG01	Login	Valid administrator login	High	Pass
RG02	Login	Wrong password rejected	High	Pass
RG03	Editor	Create page with text and image	High	Pass
RG04	Editor	Save and reopen a page	High	Pass
RG05	Media library	Upload and delete an image	Medium	Fail
RG06	Publishing	Publish and unpublish a page	High	Pass
RG07	User roles	Editor cannot delete a user	Medium	Pass
RG08	Search	Search for a published page	Low	Pass
RG09	Comments	Add and approve a comment	Medium	Fail
RG10	Backup	Take backup and restore it	High	Pass

OUTPUT
Test Execution Summary
Total Designed	Executed	Passed	Failed	Blocked	Pass %
10	10	8	2	0	80%

Regression Defect Log
Defect ID	Test Case	Description	Severity	Status
DEF-01	RG05	Image is not deleted from media library after Delete action	Major	Open
DEF-02	RG09	Approved comment is displayed twice on the page	Minor	Open

RESULT
Thus, regression testing of the content management system was executed after the update. Changed modules and dependent modules were retested, and the results were verified.
